ted issuer and actual subject that didn't align.\n\nA helper function checks if a certificate is self-signed by comparing its subject and issuer fields. The main validation function takes a list of certificates and ensures each certificate's issuer matches the next certificate's subject, returning a dictionary with validation results.\n\nThere's a critical bug in the loop range—using `range(1, len(chain))` skips the leaf-to-first-intermediate validation. The correct range should be `range(len(chain) - 1)` to check every adjacent pair. The function handles empty chains and single self-signed certificates as special cases before performing the chain validation.\n\nThe main validation loop iterates through certificate pairs, comparing each certificate's issuer against the next certificate's subject. When a mismatch is found, it records the break point with both values. Finally, it returns a dictionary indicating validity, chain depth, any validation errors, and whether the root certificate is self-signed. The fix requires two changes to the loop logic. First, change the range from `range(1, len(chain))` to `range(len(chain) - 1)` so that every adjacent pair gets validated, including the critical leaf-to-first-intermediate check. Second, simplify the conditional assignment since we're now iterating correctly—`expected = chain[i].issuer` and `actual = chain[i + 1].subject` work directly without needing the bounds check.\n\nThe corrected function validates each certificate against the next one in the chain, returning a dict with validity status, depth, any validation errors found, and whether the root is self-signed. The validation loop now correctly iterates through all adjacent certificate pairs using `range(len(chain) - 1)` instead of the buggy `range(1, len(chain))`. This ensures the leaf certificate's issuer is checked against the first intermediate's subject, catching breaks at every position in the chain. The function returns a dict indicating validity, chain depth